      "sequence_position": 58,
      "problem": "The fifth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{5}(x)=2(x-\\pi )-\\frac{4}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+\\frac{4}{15}(x-\\pi )^{5}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${f}^{(5)}(\\pi )?$",
      "worked_solution": "The coefficient of ${(x-\\pi )}^{5}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{(5)}(\\pi )}{5!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(5)}(\\pi )}{5!} & =\\frac{4}{15} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(5)}(\\pi )}{120} & =\\frac{4}{15} \\\\ {f}^{(5)}(\\pi ) & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
